  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 224 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nAs we look toward the weekend, the active pattern will continue to \nremain active as the aforementioned warm front lifts northward. As \nit does, the overall synoptic forcing looks to weaken, leading to a \ndrying trend in the afternoon. There may yet be an isolated \nthunderstorm in the area in the early morning, but with minimal \ninstability, this potential appears quite low (10-20% per the REFS). \nAs we go into Sunday, a bit more upper-level ridging will help \nlargely stave off precipitation despite some lingering atmospheric \nmoisture in place.\n\nThe main focus for the long-term continues to be on a large system \nthat's slated to translate through the central CONUS for late Monday \nthrough Wednesday, including Election Day. The culprit for this \nactive period is a high-amplitude upper-level trough and an \nassociated surface low that's progged to translate through the \nMidwest. GEFS and EC ensemble guidance suggests a tropical moisture \nconnection with this system, drawing some impressive environmental \nmoisture into the area. In fact, the LREF ensemble mean for Pwats \nare hovering around 1.6\" Monday night and Tuesday morning. If these \nvalues come to fruition during this time of year, these Pwat values \nwould be well over the daily maximum for Pwats per the SPC sounding \nclimatology. This is also backed up by the NAEFS ensemble \nclimatological percentile values, suggesting these Pwats would be \nnear the maximum of the climatology for early November. With this \nsaid, there remains uncertainty on the exact track of the surface \nlow, given significant spread of the low tracks per the EC and GEFS \nensembles. Where this surface low goes will dictate where the axis \nof the heaviest rainfall and strongest winds will be. Winds with \nthis system generally look to be around 20-25 mph, so nothing too \nimpactful. However, for areas that receive appreciable rainfall, \nthis could make for some gloomy weather conditions on Election Day. \nIn terms of rainfall amounts, there appears to be a consistent \nsignal for amounts, but due to the uncertain low track, the location \nof the axis of rainfall remains uncertain. However, the latest NBM \nexceedance probabilities of 1\" of total rain or more during this \nentire period is around 60-80% for most locations, with 2\" \nprobabilities around 30-50%.",
